Firewall gateway onboarding certificate verification
=====================================================

The Add Gateway dialog selects certificate verification by default for new
OPNsense, pfSense, MikroTik RouterOS and OpenWRT gateway connections. Both
Test Connection and Add Gateway submit that explicit choice. Operators may
uncheck verification for a device whose certificate they cannot yet validate;
the dialog shows an accessible warning about identity verification and
credential interception. Prefer a trusted certificate or an appropriately
configured backend trust store over an ongoing opt-out.

A failed connection test does not silently disable verification. Editing any
form field invalidates the displayed test result. Closing the form, including
successful creation through its parent, clears its credentials and restores
the verified default. A test response arriving after the form was changed or
closed is not shown as validation of the new form. Request rejection uses the
existing error toast and does not become an unhandled promise in this dialog.

The UI change does not rewrite existing gateway rows or edit behavior. New
create/test API requests and ORM inserts now also default to verification;
see GATEWAY-CERTIFICATE-DEFAULTS for scope and compatibility. Existing explicit
saved choices remain intact; low-level adapter defaults are not globally changed.
Certificate trust is
not a replacement for operator authorization, secure routing or least-privilege
device credentials. No successful UI test asserts vendor support across all
firmware, real appliance acceptance or independent security certification.

The regression suite drives the actual React page with API transport doubles:
secure and opted-out requests for four vendors, successful-create reset, failed
and rejected requests, changed policy/fields, late responses and cancellation.
Native packaged frontend and real-backend browser qualification remain separate
release gates. This change performs no hardware or customer gateway writes.
